{"command_id":"lo-open-timeout-cancel-20260907","action":"OPEN_MANUAL","actor":"claude-code","actor_role":"LIVE_OPS","schema_version":1,"payload":{"title":"The 4h live order timeout silently fails to enforce when its cancel raises, and the cause is undiagnosable in production","classification":"OPS","owner_role":"LIVE_OPS","severity":"HIGH","priority":"P2","problem_statement":"LiveExecutor.reconcile's per-order 4h timeout (LIVE_ORDER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS, default 14400) is a live safeguard that can stop enforcing indefinitely, silently. On 2026-09-07 one live order on the Fmmsell10 book, in a KXBTCD threshold market, was still resting 709 minutes (11.8 h) old against a 240-minute timeout, with cancel_reason NULL and zero fills. Money at risk was trivial (about $0.93); the safeguard not enforcing is not.

MECHANISM (established by reading the code, not inferred): the cancel call and the status write share one try block. A raising cancel means the row is never marked canceled, so it stays resting, so the next cycle retries it - with no attempt bound. The drain path 150 lines above in the same file has both a bound and a failure counter; the timeout path has neither. LiveCycleSummary.timed_out_canceled counts only SUCCESSES, so a book where every timeout cancel fails is indistinguishable in the cycle summary from a book with nothing to cancel.

ESTABLISHED read-only from production between 13:52Z and 14:04Z on 2026-09-07:
  - the order was still resting at 709 min and WARN 'live cancel failed' was still firing at 13:58:39Z, so the loop reaches it and the cancel raises on every cycle;
  - timeout cancels are NOT broken generally - 6 succeeded on 2026-09-07;
  - only that one order of 10 resting was past its timeout; the other 9 were under 180 min.

FALSIFIED: the leading hypothesis in the source handoff was a v1/v2 endpoint split refusing range-bucket markets. KXBTCD carries 42 successful canceled/timeout rows historically, so the v2 events cancel demonstrably works on that market family. Do not build bucket-market cancel routing for this.

NOT ESTABLISHED: why the cancel fails. The exception class and the exchange error body were written to extra_fields, which the production log endpoint drops, so production carried only the bare string 'live cancel failed'. Nothing could discriminate a 404 from a 400 from a transport error without a code change. This is the same trap already fixed twice in this file (the 429 counters, then the queue sampler) and already avoided by the drain path.

ALSO UNEXPLAINED: the warning lands in pairs about 9 seconds apart (13:26:16/13:26:25, then 13:49:08/13:49:17) while reconcile cycles run about 2.4 minutes apart. Two attempts in one cycle or two distinct callers - worth resolving rather than waving through.

A related open ticket, XOS-000014, measured Kalshi exchange sharding as the cause of user_not_found placement rejections concentrated in MLB, ITF and BTCD series, and its evidence notes that exchange_index rides on orders as well as placement. That is a plausible neighbour of this defect but is NOT claimed as its cause here: sharding is recorded there as a per-shard COLLATERAL problem, and this order was accepted onto the book and is visible to the reads. Naming it as the cause would be a guess.","reason":"Found incidentally by the WS-015 queue-aware cancellation shadow, which kept recording the same order as normal_timeout for hours. The shadow only writes audit rows and did not cause or touch this. Opened before any fix so the problem has a durable home rather than living in a handoff document and a chat window. Severity HIGH because a live safeguard is not enforcing and the failure is invisible to an operator; priority P2 because the exposure involved is trivial and the safeguard works on every other order observed."}}
